A||||6||mshill||Questions for all  you rebuilders...||||5||
Z||000000||mshill||01-16-2000||06:30 PM||||I have my engine apart. I was doing some inspection prior to removing the cam, crank and pistons. BTW, this is an 86SE V6 that has 189,000 miles on it, and I am not sure if the engine was previously rebuilt. <P>The pistons are caked with carbon.  I guess that $6 bottle that claimed it would remove the carbon from the combustion chambers didn't work, huh. I figured that with that many miles on the engine I would have a noticable ridge at the top of the engine.  There was none.  Additionally, the honing scratch pattern was still visible in the cylinder walls.  Is it likely that this engine had been rebuilt previously?<P>What is the best way to clean the carbon and gasket material off of the heads, block and manifolds?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||lowCG||01-16-2000||10:59 PM||||Does anyone know if these motors came with chrome piston rings? That just sounds like a cylinder with chrome rings to me.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||01-16-2000||11:55 PM||||If you can clean up the top of one of the pistons, see if there is a number stamped there, like 030 or something like that. Oversized pistons have the size stamped on the top. If it has oversized pistons, then it's been bored out and rebuilt. Also look at the counter weights on the crank. A stamped number like 010/010 means the mains and the rod journals have been turned down 0.010" (10 thousands), hence a rebuilt engine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||862M4inCA||01-17-2000||01:59 AM||||It's possible that the engine had EXCELENT maintainence also. A couple years ago I had to rebuild an old Toyota motor with about 160K on it and when I pulled the pistons I notice almost no ridge and even the crosshatch pattern was still quite visible. I know for a fact that the motor was never rebuilt prior to that, and that the oil and other maintainence items were changed at frequent intervals. Just a thought. As for cleaning the thing up, when I replaced my head I just used a gasket scraper and lightly used a wire brush being careful not to scratch the surface up too bad.<p>[This message has been edited by 862M4inCA (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||geyser||01-17-2000||02:18 AM||||The BEST way to clean the carbon etc... off the parts is to take them to a auto machine shop and have them do it for you. The cost vs your labor is well worth it. While you have the parts at the shop, have them check the cylinders and pistons for wear. They have all the right tools and can do the job quickly. I have all the tools and I still have them get me the baseline numbers prior to any work. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||dogtired||01-17-2000||09:26 AM||||If there is no ridge and the hone marks are still visible? Wouldn't that mean that the rings are bad? Perhaps just ring it and go on??? Just a thought!<BR>Why did you tear it down???||||reg||6
Z||000006||mshill||01-17-2000||12:30 PM||||862M4inCA,  I have a feeling that the car has been maintain extremely well, since when I got the car the engine compartment was very clean, and the front compartment and interior were nearly immaculate (like new).<P><BR>dogtired,  The rings are likely OK since compression was between 145-150 psi across all six cylinders.<P>I was droping the motor to replace the clutch and weld the exhaust manifolds.  Since it has nearly 190K miles on it and I am owner #3 (ie. basically unknown condition)  I decided to play it safe and at a minimum replace all bearings, re-ring, performance cam, lifters, pushrods, and have a valve job.<P>||||reg||1
